0. DEFINITIONS. A "toe" is an extension. A "shoe" is a theme. A "sock" is a
   third-party library. The "sole" is the rendering engine, because it is the
   part the Software actually stands on. The "arch" is whatever is holding the
   rest of it up, and nobody involved is entirely sure what that is.

1. The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in
   all copies or substantial portions of the Software, and shall remain legible
   at arm's length, which for the purposes of this License is measured in feet.

2. THE SOFTWARE SHALL BE RUN BAREFOOT. Linking against a third-party library
   constitutes wearing socks. Where the Software requires a JPEG decoder, a
   font rasteriser, a regular expression engine or an H.264 decoder, the
   Licensee shall grow one. "There is a crate for that" is not a defence and
   shall not be pleaded.

3. Every new subsystem shall be named after a part of the foot. The Licensee is
   granted broad latitude as to the heel, the ball and the instep, but shall
   not name anything after the ankle, which is a leg part, and everybody knows
   it.

4. No fork of the Software shall exceed five (5) toes. A sixth toe may be
   merged only with the written consent of a podiatrist and at least two
   reviewers, in that order.

5. Shoes are permitted, and indeed encouraged (see about:shoes). Shoes worn
   with socks are not; see condition 2. The Licensee may distribute the
   Software in sandals only if prepared to defend that decision publicly.

6. Where two implementation languages are available and one of them is the
   obvious choice, the Licensee shall put their foot down and select the other
   one, provided it benchmarks within one order of magnitude of C. Fortran is
   expressly blessed. COBOL was measured at 1.3 Mbin/s and is expressly not.

7. The Software is distributed unsigned. Gatekeeper's opinion of the Software
   is noted, respected, and stepped over.

8. Rowhammer remains an open issue. Any resulting bit flip shall be construed
   as a stubbed toe: agonising, entirely the Licensee's own fault, and over in
   about a minute.

